- observed_method: trigger_table_clean.csv, 55 series 2022-2026, 179 players, 25 teams, min 10 poss.\n- observed_verdict: descriptive\n- observed_failure_type: E_unclear\n- observed_notes: NOT_RECORDED\n- auto_confidence: medium (0.65)\n- auto_addresses_question: RQC:RQC-001"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-APPS-043", "score": 0.082, "document": "# FAM:SF-APPS-043 | Study family SF-APPS-043: PNR suppression RTM-excess team and player tables\n- observed_research_question: PNR suppression RTM-excess team and player tables\n- auto_outcome_status: not a hypothesis test\n- observed_hypothesis: PNR H1->H2 drops contain a team- and player-specific RTM-excess signal (who loses PNR; who takes it away).\n- observed_result: Offense excess c=0.10: GSW +0.702 n=12; MIA +0.110 CI[-0.119,+0.340] n=15. Defense: DAL +0.761 n=6 (CI crosses 0); UTA -0.005 CI[-0.203,+0.194] n=7. Players c=0.10 n>=5: J. Brown +0.643 n=10; only 2 players n>=10 at this [cut; read_family for the full text]\n- auto_failure_mechanism: A_unanswerable (question not answerable with the data or design)\n- observed_failure_evidence: Overlapping CIs; player n>=10 at primary caliper is 2; many team CIs include 0. Ranks not distinguishable.\n- observed_sample_and_power: 380 matched pairs at c=0.10 from 2838 triggers\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- observed_verdict: descriptive"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-APPA-044", "score": 0.077, "document": "# FAM:SF-APPA-044 | Study family SF-APPA-044: Oracle player matchup specificity on BEST counters (Route step)\n- observed_research_question: Oracle player matchup specificity on BEST counters (Route step)\n- auto_outcome_status: supported\n- observed_hypothesis: The oracle BEST player for a counter play type varies by opponent, not a single team identity.\n- observed_result: 64/78 (82%) (off, PT) cells have >1 oracle player across opponents; 19/78 dominated by one player (>=90%); median oracle-player entropy 0.928.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: D_positive (no failure: positive result)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:small_sample\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER\n- observed_failure_evidence: 82% of cells vary the oracle player by opponent.\n- observed_sample_and_power: n=6,852 oracle BEST possessions; 78 cells\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- auto_related_family: FAM:SF-APPA-045\n- auto_related_family: FAM:SF06\n- observed_method: _oracle_player_specificity.py; n=6,852 oracle BEST possessions; (off_team, play_type) cells."}], "n_results": 4}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
 "research_concepts": [
  "Player absence (L)",
  "Offensive reorganization (relative to L)",
  "Change in points per possession",
  "Predictive relationship"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "How to define and measure 'L' (player absence)?",
  "How to quantify 'offensive reorganization'?",
  "How to measure 'change in points per possession' accurately for a team?",
  "What is the appropriate unit of analysis for this study?",
  "How to control for confounding variables (e.g., opponent strength, game context, other player absences, sample size of possessions)?"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-088",
   "status_as_recorded": "supported",
   "finding": "Connector absence displaces team offensive identity (D_A) more than usage-matched non-organizer absence. Unmatched conn vs role D_A 0.2020 vs 0.1021, t=5.97 p=2.87e-08. Usage-matched vs role: paired dD_A +0.1151, P(conn>ctrl)=81.2%, t=6.35 p=7.35e-09 n=96. Vs terminal: +0.0777 t=2.37 p=0.0261 n=25."
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "The concept of 'offensive reorganization' as a measurable phenomenon related to player absence is valid and has been empirically supported (FAM:SF-APPS-088). Specifically, the absence of 'connector' players causes a significant displacement in team offensive identity (D_A).",
  "Previous work has successfully identified and quantified 'offensive identity displacement' (D_A) as a metric for reorganization."
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "The specific predictive relationship between the *magnitude* of offensive reorganization and the *change* in points per possession has not been established.",
  "How 'offensive reorganization' will be measured in this specific study (though D_A is a strong candidate) is not specified by the historical evidence.",
  "The optimal definition and criteria for 'L' (player absence) for the current research question are not specified.",
  "Methodological approaches for isolating the causal effect or predictive power of reorganization on points per possession, while controlling for confounders, are not detailed in the retrieved evidence.",
  "The historical evidence does not address the specific challenges of measuring 'change in points per possession' or the appropriate unit of analysis for such a change."
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "The research question is testable given the historical evidence that offensive reorganization is measurable.",
  "The 'offensive identity displacement' (D_A) metric from FAM:SF-APPS-088 is a strong candidate for measuring 'how much the offense reorganizes'.",
  "Defining 'L' (player absence) will be a critical step, likely requiring a clear threshold for minutes missed or games missed by key players.",
  "The 'change in points per possession' will likely need to be calculated by comparing team performance in games with 'L' to games without 'L', or to a baseline performance."
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "The precise definition of 'L' (player absence) for this study, including player type, duration of absence, and number of absent players.",
  "The specific metric for 'offensive reorganization' to be used (e.g., D_A or another metric based on play-type distributions).",
  "The specific methodology for calculating 'change in points per possession' and handling small sample sizes of possessions in individual game segments.",
  "The extent to which confounding factors (e.g., opponent, game score, other injuries, coaching adjustments) can be effectively controlled.",
  "The exact statistical power achievable with the stated sample sizes (n=986 games, n=148 spells)."
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes",
  "explanation": "The historical evidence, particularly FAM:SF-APPS-088, confirmed that 'offensive reorganization' is a measurable and responsive concept to player absence (specifically 'connector' absence). This strengthens confidence in the testability of the 'how much the offense reorganizes' part of the question. Without this evidence, the feasibility of measuring 'offensive reorganization' would have been a significant blocking constraint."
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.8,
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
    "text": "While n=986 games and n=148 spells are provided, the effective sample size for specific player absences and sufficient 'pre/post' or 'with/without' observations for robust reorganization and efficiency change measurements might be limited. FAM:SF-APPS-088 was limited by 'small_sample' and 'SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER' even with n=96 matched pairs."
   },
   {
    "code": "CONFOUNDING",
    "text": "Player absence is often correlated with other factors (e.g., opponent strength, coaching changes, other player health issues, schedule difficulty) that could also impact points per possession, making it challenging to isolate the effect of reorganization alone."
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "partly",
  "cited": [
   "FAM:SF-APPS-088"
  ],
  "summary": "FAM:SF-APPS-088 established that offensive identity (a form of reorganization) is significantly displaced by the absence of 'connector' players. This confirms the measurability and existence of offensive reorganization in response to player absence. However, it did not connect the magnitude of this reorganization to changes in points per possession, which is the core of the current predictive question."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "The number of meaningful 'L' events (player absences) with sufficient pre- and post-absence data to reliably measure both reorganization and changes in points per possession might be low, leading to underpowered tests. The stated 'n=148 spells' might still be insufficient for granular analysis per player/team."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Many factors can affect points per possession beyond offensive reorganization due to a specific player's absence. These include opponent strength, game context (score, period), other simultaneous player absences, coaching adjustments, and natural performance variance. Isolating the effect of reorganization from these confounders will be critical and challenging."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The reliability and sensitivity of the chosen 'offensive reorganization' metric (e.g., D_A or similar) and the 'change in points per possession' measure, especially over potentially small sample sizes of possessions, need to be carefully considered. 'Offensive identity' metrics might be subject to 'MECHANICAL_FLOOR' risks as noted in FAM:SF-APPS-088."
  },
  {
   "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
   "text": "If 'L' events are selected based on observed drops in performance or other criteria, there's a risk of regression to the mean biasing the observed 'change in points per possession'."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.4,
  "rejected": 0.2,
  "untestable": 0.1,
  "artifact": 0.1,
  "uncertain": 0.2
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of the magnitude of offensive reorganization (relative to player L's absence) on the subsequent change in team points per possession.",
  "unit": "Player-absence spell. A spell is defined as a contiguous set of games where player L is absent, preceded and followed by games where L is present. Each spell will be associated with a team.",
  "sample": "All player-absence spells (n=148) for the 2022-23 to 2025-26 NBA regular seasons and playoffs. Player L must be a starter or high-usage (top 5 in minutes/usage for their team). Exclude absences shorter than 3 games or where multiple key players are absent simultaneously. Minimum 10 games played by player L before and after the absence spell for baseline calculation.",
  "power": "To be determined via simulation. The primary unit of analysis will be the player-absence spell. We will aim for an effect size that corresponds to a meaningful change in offensive efficiency (e.g., 0.02 points per possession for a 0.1 increase in reorganization metric). Given n=148 spells, power may be limited for granular analysis.",
  "controls": "Match each absence spell with a control period for the same team where player L is present, controlling for opponent strength (e.g., using ELO ratings or similar), game location, and season. Alternatively, use a difference-in-differences approach comparing performance before and during absence. Confounding variables to control for include: opponent defensive efficiency, game pace, other simultaneous player absences (if not already excluded by sample definition), and coach changes. Use team-fixed effects to account for baseline team offensive identity.",
  "falsification": "Examine if 'reorganization' predicts changes in defensive efficiency (should not, or less strongly). Test if 'reorganization' measured randomly (not tied to player absence) predicts points per possession changes. Test if reorganization for non-organizer players (as defined in FAM:SF-APPS-088) has a weaker predictive relationship.",
  "multiplicity": "No multiple testing correction needed as this is a single, pre-specified hypothesis. If multiple definitions of 'reorganization' or 'L' are explored, adjust p-values using Bonferroni or FDR correction.",
  "decision_rule": "The hypothesis will be supported if the regression coefficient for 'offensive reorganization' predicting 'change in points per possession' is statistically significant (p < 0.05) and positive, with a meaningful effect size, after controlling for confounders.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 regular season and playoffs data. Play-by-play, substitution, starters, defensive matchup, box-score data. Derived play-type possession records."
 }

